sed out what it does mean is you've  lost all your sensory perception gray  out is where you start to lose the  eyesight first the issue comes if you do  a rapid onset of G and you weren't  prepared  you may go straight through grayer into  black out into G lot and then you have  issues a bit like with the red arrows  gonna form us a few years ago in 2011 a  member of the RAF display team was  killed when his Hawk jet crashed an  inquest concluded that John egging had  probably fallen unconscious during the  flight unlike Shoreham no one else was  killed or injured a cockpit camera  suggested Andy Hill was conscious during  his routine but his defense successfully  argued he must have been cognitively  impaired it would explain certainly the  unusual part of the maneuver over the  top and the lack of response until the  airplane was pointing 90 degrees down  had Andy's been fully conscious and  aware I would have expected he would  have spotted that the height at the top  of the maneuver was incorrect and he  should have then easily been able to fly  an escape maneuver and then carry on  with this display or stop altogether  looking for the right height for an  experienced pilots a loupe is a  relatively straightforward maneuver but  they're also trained on how to get out  of trouble if something goes wrong we  asked mark to demonstrate what happens  if you haven't gained sufficient height  or speed the escape maneuver is called  unloading and rolling out safely in a  different direction  Vande Hill failed to do this compound in  his original error at the beginning of  his loop Mark Petrie says Shoreham can  be a tricky airfield to display over  because the runway dual-carriageway  coastline and river are in close  proximity but under normal circumstances  a pilot should be able to adapt to an  altitude error you have sufficient time  if you are fully conscious and aware of  what's going on around you  and it's part of the process of doing  aerobatic maneuvers especially in a high  powered aeroplane that you should be  checking at specific points that you've  met the gate requirements and then be  ready to respond to it if when you look  at it the numbers are wrong the  prosecution had argued that mr. Hill had  made other errors at air shows included  at this display in Southport in 2014 the  organizer issued an emergency  stopstopstop call because he felt mr.  Hill had flown too close to spectators  he admitted the mistake but his friends  defended his record I've never known him  take any risks he amongst many others  including myself will have made small  errors during the course of our flying  careers but he was a very disciplined a  very focused individual took a great  deal of interest in the detail of every  sortie when he gave evidence in his own  defense he talked about the tragedy  tried to explain his actions but didn't  apologize to families of those who died  who were in  court we all felt that andrew hill has  to take responsibility for what had  happened they were well very lengthy  pieces of video footage that we sat  through and it was almost like a master  class in this is andrew hill and how he  actually flies and perhaps his lawyers  had told him not to say anything about  any sort of remorse but it just seemed  that it was almost like he was lecturing  us rather than taking as I said  responsibility for it but an hour after  he was found not guilty mr. Hill emerged  from court to deliver the apology the  families had waited so long to hear 1290  five days ago I lost control of an  aircraft resulting in the death of  Morris Abraham's Dylan Archer  Tony bright well James Mallinson Matthew  Grimstone Matt Jones Danny any poly tube  pollito mark Reeth Jacob Schultz Richard  Smith mark Trotter a number of people  were injured I'm truly sorry for the  part I played in their death today that  I will remember for the rest of my life  understandably the Shoreham disaster had  a profound impact on air displays  hundreds of thousands of spectators  flocked to see air shows in Britain and  Ireland but the Civil Aviation Authority  tightened the rules to stop fast jets  performing over populated areas hawker  hunters are still banned from any  aerobatics if that means that people no  longer feel comfortable displaying to  their personal limit if that would norm  at normally include loops barrel rolls  and other  Hayate what we consider to be  high-energy maneuvers I think that would  be a shame for the public because part  of the spectacle is to display these  aircraft in the context of what they  were designed for and if all of a sudden  we only feel inclined for personal risk  and other reasons to fry them straight  past the crowd straight and level  I think the nations lost something but  it might be safer it's safe to leave  them on the ground  despite the disaster of those in charge  of British air shows want to see the  Hawker hunter flying again put yourself  in the shoes of a family who've lost  somebody to this accident how would you  feel if you were looking in the skies  and saw another Hawker hunter doing a  aleut maneuver in front of you it would  be extremely difficult and I'm sure a  very emotional experience but and indeed  of course it is intrinsically personal  but one would hope that they would never  ever ever see an incident like that  again organisers of air shows have  argued that mr. hills crash was an  isolated tragic mistake they were the  first public fatalities at an air  display since 31 died at the Farnborough  airshow in 1952 but that's of little  comfort to the families of the men who  died in such a horrific way Andy hill  may have no memory of the crash but the  bereaved relatives will never forget his  catastrophic error
 

